  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "⚖️ Law Firm Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a law firm or solo practice, from Firm Footing Family Law to Hartley & Cobb."
    },
    "intro": "Name your practice with this **Law Firm Name Generator**: for solicitors' firms, attorneys' offices and solo lawyers. Pick a style and practice area, or add your first name or town.",
    "sections": [],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do I check a law firm name is free?",
        "answer": "Press Check on any name to see whether it's free as a company, a domain and a social media handle. In the UK, search Companies House; in the US, search your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. Check your local area too, as two similar names nearby confuse customers."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I name my law firm?",
        "answer": "Most firms use the founders' surnames (\"Hartley & Cobb\", \"Hartley Law\"), which clients trust and which grows with you. A solo lawyer can use their own name or the town; a practice that does one thing, such as family or immigration law, does well to say so. Keep it plain and honest: a name must never promise results or suggest a size you aren't."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I put \"Solicitors\" or \"Attorneys\" in my firm's name?",
        "answer": "Only if you are entitled to. In England and Wales, only a firm authorised by the Solicitors Regulation Authority may call itself \"Solicitors\", and \"barrister\" and \"chambers\" belong to the Bar. \"Law\", \"Legal\" and \"Lawyers\" are open words, but a business must not give the impression it is regulated when it isn't. In the US, each state bar sets the rules for firm names: most allow trade names that aren't misleading, \"Attorneys\" must mean licensed lawyers, and \"& Associates\" should only be used if you have associates. That is why names here leave those words out: add them yourself if they apply to you. Immigration advice and claims handling are regulated too, and \"Trust\" and \"Chartered\" need approval in a UK company name."
      }
    ]
  },
